Relevant column(s) and their possible use  
• Movie.year: movie release year – basis for all period calculations.  
• Movie.MID (or Movie.index) – can be used to count rows, make sure we only look at real movies.  
Conditions that may be useful  
• WHERE Movie.year IS NOT NULL                – ignore missing years.  
• CAST(Movie.year AS INTEGER) BETWEEN …       – treat year as an integer when comparing.  

Below are ten “exploratory → increasingly‑complex → final answer” queries, each annotated.  
All queries use LIMIT 20 so that you can run them safely on large tables first.  

```sql
-- 1. Description: show a handful of distinct years available in the data
SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS "yr"
FROM "Movie"
WHERE "year" IS NOT NULL
ORDER BY "yr"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: count how many movies were released in each individual year
SELECT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS "yr",
       COUNT(*)                 AS "movie_cnt"
FROM   "Movie"
WHERE  "year" IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY CAST("year" AS INTEGER)
ORDER  BY "movie_cnt" DESC, "yr"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: show the smallest and largest release years in the table
SELECT MIN(CAST("year" AS INTEGER)) AS "earliest",
       MAX(CAST("year" AS INTEGER)) AS "latest"
FROM   "Movie";
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: list every distinct start‑year that could launch a 10‑year window
SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS "start_year"
FROM   "Movie"
ORDER  BY "start_year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: for one concrete start year (e.g. 2010) count movies in 2010‑2019
SELECT 2010                                               AS "start_year",
       COUNT(*)                                           AS "movies_in_window"
FROM   "Movie"
WHERE  "year" IS NOT NULL
  AND  CAST("year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN 2010 AND 2019;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: same as #5 but parametric – use a correlated sub‑query
--                 to show each start year and its 10‑year total
SELECT s."start_year",
       ( SELECT COUNT(*)
         FROM   "Movie" m2
         WHERE  m2."year" IS NOT NULL
           AND  CAST(m2."year" AS INTEGER)
                BETWEEN s."start_year" AND s."start_year" + 9
       ) AS "movies_in_window"
FROM  (
        SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS "start_year"
        FROM   "Movie"
      ) AS s
ORDER BY "movies_in_window" DESC, "start_year"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: find the single start year whose 10‑year span has the max count
SELECT s."start_year",
       COUNT(m2."MID") AS "movies_in_window"
FROM  (
        SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS "start_year"
        FROM   "Movie"
      ) AS s
JOIN  "Movie" m2
      ON CAST(m2."year" AS INTEGER)
         BETWEEN s."start_year" AND s."start_year" + 9
GROUP BY s."start_year"
ORDER BY "movies_in_window" DESC, s."start_year"
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: same as #7 but explicitly cast once in a CTE for clarity
WITH years AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS y
  FROM   "Movie"
)
SELECT y AS "start_year",
       (SELECT COUNT(*)
        FROM   "Movie"
        WHERE  CAST("year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN y AND y+9) AS "movies_in_window"
FROM   years
ORDER  BY "movies_in_window" DESC, y
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: verify the total number of movies contained in that winning window
WITH winner AS (
  /* pick the start year with the most movies */
  SELECT y AS "start_year"
  FROM  (
          SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS y FROM "Movie"
        ) AS yrs
  ORDER BY (SELECT COUNT(*)
            FROM   "Movie"
            WHERE  CAST("year" AS INTEGER) BETWEEN y AND y+9) DESC,
           y
  LIMIT 1
)
SELECT w."start_year",
       COUNT(*) AS "movies_in_window"
FROM   "Movie" m
JOIN   winner w
WHERE  CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER)
       BETWEEN w."start_year" AND w."start_year" + 9
GROUP  BY w."start_year";
```

```sql
-- 10. Description (final answer query): compact form that directly returns the start
--     year and total # of movies for the busiest 10‑year period in the data
SELECT s."start_year",
       COUNT(m."MID") AS "movies_in_window"
FROM  (
        SELECT DISTINCT CAST("year" AS INTEGER) AS "start_year"
        FROM   "Movie"
        WHERE  "year" IS NOT NULL
      ) AS s
JOIN  "Movie" m
      ON CAST(m."year" AS INTEGER)
         BETWEEN s."start_year" AND s."start_year" + 9
GROUP BY s."start_year"
ORDER BY "movies_in_window" DESC, s."start_year"
LIMIT 1;
```

